1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,804
Five young men in a practice room, dancing until two in the morning.

2
00:00:09,204 --> 00:00:12,917
Nobody knew their names yet. Not even the mop in the corner.

3
00:00:14,217 --> 00:00:21,387
In twenty sixteen, a Korean entertainment company, ShowBT, came to Manila looking for future stars.

4
00:00:21,787 --> 00:00:28,657
Their plan: train Filipino performers the K-pop way. Singing, dancing, and endless practice.

5
00:00:29,057 --> 00:00:35,395
Trainees came and went. In the end, five stayed: Pablo, Josh, Stell, Ken, and Justin.

6
00:00:36,695 --> 00:00:41,944
They trained for about three years. Same songs, same steps, again and again.

7
00:00:42,344 --> 00:00:46,441
Some days the only audience was a wall of mirrors. And the mop.

8
00:00:47,741 --> 00:00:54,783
On October twenty-sixth, twenty eighteen, SB19 finally debuted with a ballad called Tilaluha.

9
00:00:55,183 --> 00:00:58,704
Hardly anyone noticed. It was a very quiet start.

10
00:01:00,004 --> 00:01:06,107
In twenty nineteen they released Go Up, and posted a video of themselves practicing the dance.

11
00:01:06,507 --> 00:01:11,884
That practice video started spreading online. Share after share, view after view.

12
00:01:12,284 --> 00:01:17,341
All those late nights of practice were suddenly on phones all over the country.

13
00:01:18,641 --> 00:01:26,045
In November twenty nineteen, SB19 became the first Filipino act on Billboard's Next Big Sound chart.

14
00:01:26,445 --> 00:01:32,463
Weeks later, they entered the Billboard Social 50. Their fans, called A'TIN, made sure of it.

15
00:01:32,863 --> 00:01:39,157
In twenty twenty-one, they were even nominated for Top Social Artist at the Billboard Music Awards.

16
00:01:40,457 --> 00:01:45,515
Then came bigger songs like Alab and What?, and arenas glowing with lightsticks.

17
00:01:45,915 --> 00:01:51,740
So how did SB19 become stars? Not overnight. About three years of late nights.
